Let $q$ be a highest secondary peak for $H$ in $\kappa$.
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $q-a$ is a highest secondary peak for $H-a$ in $i\mapsto\kappa_{i+a}$;
    \item $-q$ is a highest secondary peak for $-H$ in $i\mapsto\kappa_{-i}$;
    \item for $s>0$, $q$ is a highest secondary peak for $H$ in $\kappa/s$;
    \item if $\kappa_q>0$, then $0$ is a highest secondary peak for $H-q$ in
      $\mu=\operatorname{normalizedAt}(\kappa,q)$, and $\mu_0=1$.
  \end{enumerate}
  Moreover, if $\kappa>0$ then $\mu>0$; if $\kappa$ is adjacent-unequal then
  so are $\mu$ (when $\kappa_q>0$) and $i\mapsto\kappa_{-i}$.
